# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two
# FILES, never a shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is
# defined in run_gates.sh. Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_py_base_class_edge.py" "PY BASE-CLASS RECALL GATE: PASS" "python base-class recall" "python base-class recall: a Python class Sub(Base) DEPENDS ON the file defining Base, but the call graph missed it — a base appears in ClassDef.bases as a Name/Attribute, never an ast.Call, so no edge was emitted; this is the SAME high-precision sym_use:base the tree-sitter spec already gives Java/C#/Rust/PHP/C++/Swift, which Python's bespoke ast path never had; MEASURED on pallets/flask: live dampened co-change recall 30.7 -> 31.3 pct / raw 40.8 -> 41.4 pct, every new live pair a REAL inheritance link, zero fabricated couplings; the base is emitted as a CONTENT-FREE calls edge to the bare base NAME so it flows through the EXACT same _claim_adjacency resolution every call passes — defs<=3 fan-out cap, import-confirmation, single-definer, hub dampening — so a multi-definer un-imported base is DROPPED not fanned out; gate proves recall (imported single-definer base WARNS), precision (multi-definer un-imported base stays CLEAR = false_coupling invariant), and a control that the recall check is load-bearing" "python base-class recall: inheritance is real cross-file coupling — a subclass + its base file must coordinate; recall-safe and precision-preserving" 28
